[long_report.txt#4] (section: Retrieval System Design Notes)
The chunking stage is where the cleaned text gets cut into pieces small enough to embed and retrieve individually. Two different strategies are implemented here so their trade-offs can be compared directly on the same input. The first strategy cuts the text into fixed-size windows measured in characters, with a small overlap between consecutive windows so that a sentence sitting on a boundary is not lost entirely from either chunk.

[long_report.txt#8] (section: Retrieval System Design Notes)
Paragraph-based chunking is attractive for the opposite reason: because it respects the author's own structure, each chunk tends to correspond to one coherent idea, which usually improves both embedding quality and the readability of retrieved snippets.

[long_report.txt#11] (section: Retrieval System Design Notes)
Fixed-size chunking remains useful as a fallback for the minority of documents that have little or no paragraph structure to lean on, or as a safety net inside the paragraph strategy itself when an individual paragraph turns out to be unusually long.

