The cleaning stage takes that raw text and makes it consistent across every source format. It normalizes Unicode so visually identical characters compare equal, repairs a handful of common encoding artifacts, and unifies line endings so downstream code never has to branch on whether a file came from Windows or Unix. Repeated running headers and footers, such as page counters, are detected and removed so they do not pollute every chunk with the same boilerplate line.

[long_report.txt#4] (section: Retrieval System Design Notes)
The chunking stage is where the cleaned text gets cut into pieces small enough to embed and retrieve individually. Two different strategies are implemented here so their trade-offs can be compared directly on the same input. The first strategy cuts the text into fixed-size windows measured in characters, with a small overlap between consecutive windows so that a sentence sitting on a boundary is not lost entirely from either chunk.

[long_report.txt#3] (section: Retrieval System Design Notes)
Runs of blank lines and repeated spaces are also collapsed down to a single blank line or a single space, which keeps chunk boundaries from landing on meaningless whitespace.

