Paragraph-based chunking is attractive for the opposite reason: because it respects the author's own structure, each chunk tends to correspond to one coherent idea, which usually improves both embedding quality and the readability of retrieved snippets.

[long_report.txt#10] (section: Retrieval System Design Notes)
For a real corpus made up of normal prose documents with visible paragraph structure, such as reports, articles, and documentation pages, paragraph-based chunking is the better default because retrieval quality benefits more from semantically coherent chunks than from perfectly uniform chunk sizes.

[long_report.txt#6] (section: Retrieval System Design Notes)
Fixed-size chunking is attractive because it is trivial to implement, its output sizes are extremely predictable, and it works even on text that has no discernible paragraph structure at all, such as a wall of OCR output or a transcript with no line breaks.

